<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Camino Francés</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as French Way</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Camino Francés is the principal route of the pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>, crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> at Roncesvalles and running through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puente-la-reina">Puente la Reina</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a> and León to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>. It developed from the eleventh century with the support of the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, who settled Frankish traders along it. Part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, it remains one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s most travelled pilgrim roads.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/camino-frances.jpg" alt="Camino Francés" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: WikiPate · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_French_Way.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The French Way follows the long-distance footpath GR 65 and is the most popular of the routes of the Way of St. James. It begins at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-jean-pied-de-port">Saint-Jean-Pied-de-Port</a> on the French side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, crosses to Roncesvalles and runs another 780 kilometres to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/logrono">Logroño</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a> and León. Walking the whole route usually takes at least four weeks, with one or two rest days on the way.</p><p>Paths from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>, Vézelay and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-puy-en-velay">Le Puy-en-Velay</a> meet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-jean-pied-de-port">Saint-Jean-Pied-de-Port</a>. A fourth French route, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arles">Arles</a> in Provence, crosses the frontier between Somport and Canfranc, follows the Aragonese Way and joins the French Way at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puente-la-reina">Puente la Reina</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>, about 700 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In 1993 the French Way and the Spanish routes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camino-de-santiago">Camino de Santiago</a> were inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List, for their importance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> as a major pilgrimage route and as a testimony to the exchange of ideas and cultures along them.</p><p>The Pilgrim's Office in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> counts the pilgrims who receive its certificate. In 2024 over 235,000 of them, about 47 per cent, took the French Way. Most began in Sarria, and 14 per cent at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-jean-pied-de-port">Saint-Jean-Pied-de-Port</a>, where nine in ten came from neither <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> nor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Of the 2024 pilgrims on the route, 94 per cent travelled on foot and 2 per cent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bicycle">bicycle</a>; about 300 rode horses and 170 travelled in wheelchairs, and most finished between May and September.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “French Way” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By favouring the pilgrimage route to Santiago and its Frankish settlers, Navarre helped tie northern Spain to the Romanesque culture and church reform of France; the route, the <b>Camino Francés</b>, remains one of the most travelled pilgrim roads in Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
